// @run
// @log 3
// @log 1
// A type PARAMETER whose name shadows a builtin (`function id<i32>(v: i32)`,
// `type Box<i32> = { v: i32 }`) binds NOTHING — the checker resolves the annotation to
// the builtin, which is why `Box<string>` is reported as `{v: i32}`. The emitter must
// rule the same way, and `genTyParamArr` is where it says so.
//
// Without that agreement the arm swallows a GENUINE `i32[]` annotation elsewhere in the
// program, so `collectA` never forces the i32 list's wrapper + backing and the list
// valtype dangles: INVALID WASM on a program `vl check` accepts (rc=0, then
// `failed to compile: wasm[0]::function[…]`). That was live on master for the FuncDecl
// half — the `id<i32>` line below alone reproduced it — and extending the arm to
// generic aliases would have opened the same hole a second time. The guard is
// `checkArrName`'s own accept set read as a predicate: a name that call would ACCEPT
// stays with it, because it is the arm that forces the heap types.
//
// `n` and `mk` are exported so nothing else in the program mentions an i32 list — an
// array LITERAL would force the types by itself and the case would prove nothing.
function id<i32>(v: i32) { return v }

type Box<i32> = { v: i32 }

export function n(xs: i32[]) { return xs.length }

export function mk(b: Box<i32>) { return b.v }

const b: Box<i32> = { v: 1 }
print(id(3))
print(b.v)
